diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the reported outage. End-to-end connectivity between the two PCs is healthy now. From pc0 to pc12, ICMP echo succeeded consistently with low latency (rtt ~0.8–1.1 ms), and the topology-wide check confirmed bidirectional reachability. On the routing boundary, r1 (pc0’s default gateway) is learning 120.0.0.0/8 via OSPF from r11: “show ip route 120.1.1.10” on r1 points to next-hop 111.1.1.2 over eth1 (the r1–r11 link). On the far side, r12 (pc12’s gateway) has 120.0.0.0/8 directly connected on eth1 (segment GG), and pc12 uses 120.1.1.1 as its gateway. This shows the OSPF↔EIGRP redistribution at r11/r12 is functioning: r12 advertises 120/8 via EIGRP to r11, which redistributes it into OSPF toward r1, and r1 sends traffic back the same way.

If redistribution between the “different routing” domains had failed, r1 would lack a route to 120/8 and pings would time out; that’s not the case. The one ping invocation that “timed out” was due to a tool execution limit, not packet loss.

Root cause: No persistent network fault found; the earlier failure was transient or non-network (e.g., brief IGP convergence or host-side issue) and has cleared.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Recommend monitoring for recurrence; if observed, capture “show ip route 120.0.0.0/8” on r1 and r11 and EIGRP/OSPF neighbor status on r11/r12 at the time to pinpoint a transient routing flap. === END ===